 ## Background
-The Path Integral Framework was introduced by Eric Veach in his 1997 thesis and further developed by Pauly et al. in 2000 for volumetric light transport [S2][S6, unofficial]. It reformulates the recursive rendering equation into a non-recursive integral over path space, derived via von Neumann series expansion [S6, unofficial]. This formulation has become the theoretical basis for modern path tracing and unidirectional/bidirectional integrators in computer graphics [S2, S3]. It is widely used in research on differentiable rendering, NLOS imaging, and robust volumetric simulation [S1][S4, S6, unofficial].
+The Path Integral Framework was introduced by Eric Veach in his 1997 thesis and further developed by Pauly et al. in 2000 for volumetric light transport [S2][S6, unofficial]. It reformulates the recursive rendering equation into a non-recursive integral over path space, derived via von Neumann series expansion [S6, unofficial]. This formulation has become the theoretical basis for modern path tracing and unidirectional/bidirectional integrators in computer graphics [S2, S3]. It is applied in NLOS imaging [S1], differentiable rendering [S4, unofficial], and robust volumetric simulation [S6, unofficial].
 
